<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="<%= theme.followingSystem ? 'system' : theme.id %>">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title><%= title %> - forgeright</title>
    <%- include('partials/theme-head') %>
</head>
<body>
<%#
    ONE VIEW FOR EVERY STATUS, and the chrome is a FLAG rather than a second file.

    WHY ONE AND NOT TWO. A 421 fires for a request whose Host this installation does not
    answer for, and at that moment the server has not read the cookie or resolved the theme -
    see the note in server.js. A second view would need the same fallback work in both places,
    and the two would drift the first time a status was added to one. The differences between
    the console's page and the visitor's are small: a line of context, and where the one link
    goes. A flag says that in the markup, where it can be read.

    WHY `title` AND `heading` ARE SEPARATE. The tab title is what somebody scanning several
    open tabs sees, so it names the status plainly ("Not found"); the heading is the sentence
    they read once they are on the page, which can say what to do about it.

    WHY THERE IS NO STACK, PATH OR CONFIG HERE. Every value on this page is a literal in the
    view or a status we chose; nothing is interpolated from the request beyond the status
    itself. An error page is the last place to leak a file path or a variable name, because it
    is the one page a stranger is most likely to reach on purpose.
%>
<div class="wrap narrow">
    <header>
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">forgeright</p>
            <h1><%= heading %></h1>
        </div>
        <div class="switchers">
            <%- include('partials/theme-picker') %>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="problem"><%= message %></div>

    <% if (typeof detail !== 'undefined' && detail) { %>
        <p class="meta"><%= detail %></p>
    <% } %>

    <p><a href="<%= basePath %><%= backPath %>"><%= backLabel %></a></p>
</div>
</body>
</html>
